.TH EVQ 1 "2026-09-20" "caveutils" "caveutils Manual"
.SH NAME
evq \- a tiny file-backed event queue for shell scripts.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B evq push [-q NAME] [EVENT...]
.br
.B evq pop [-q NAME] [-w [SECS]]
.br
.B evq peek [-q NAME]
.br
.B evq list [-q NAME]
.br
.B evq count [-q NAME]
.br
.B evq listen [-q NAME] CMD...
.br
.B evq clear [-q NAME]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B evq
.nf

Named queues live as one file per event under
${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/tmp}/evq-$UID/NAME/ (default queue: "default").
Push writes atomically (tmp + rename); pop claims the oldest event with
an atomic rename, so any number of producers and consumers can share a
queue without locks and no event is delivered twice. Events are FIFO
by nanosecond timestamp. An event is one line of text
or, with stdin, whatever stdin holds.

.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B push [-q NAME] [EVENT...]
append EVENT (or stdin) to the queue
.TP
.B pop [-q NAME] [-w [SECS]]
print and remove the oldest event; -w blocks until one arrives (or SECS pass, exit 1)
.TP
.B peek [-q NAME]
print the oldest event without removing it
.TP
.B list [-q NAME]
print all pending events, oldest first
.TP
.B count [-q NAME]
print the number of pending events
.TP
.B listen [-q NAME] CMD...
run CMD with each event as its last argument, forever
.TP
.B clear [-q NAME]
drop every pending event
